        <div id="cinnamon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Does Horchata Protein Powder Use Real Cinnamon?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two of the five picks list real cinnamon on the label: Orgain lists organic cinnamon powder, and Primeval Labs lists organic cinnamon specifically as Cinnamomum burmannii, which is Indonesian cassia. MusclePharm and LGXNDS list only natural and artificial or natural flavors, and we could not see ProDough's Horchata ingredient list. The discontinued Jocko Molk Horchata also listed cinnamon.
          </p>

          <p>
            The type of cinnamon matters if you drink a shake every day. Cassia contains far more coumarin than Ceylon cinnamon, and coumarin in large daily amounts can stress the liver in sensitive people. Our <a href="/best-cinnamon-protein-powder/">best cinnamon protein powder guide</a> explains the difference and why a quarter teaspoon of cassia a day is a sensible ceiling for most adults. The cinnamon in one scoop of a flavored powder is a small fraction of a teaspoon, so a daily horchata shake is not a coumarin concern on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            It becomes one if you stack sources. Horchata shakes invite extra cinnamon on top, cinnamon in oatmeal, and cinnamon coffee. If you add your own, Ceylon is the safer choice, and anyone with liver disease or who takes medicines processed by the liver should ask a doctor before using cinnamon daily.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavor without cinnamon on the label is not a defect. A natural flavor can be built from cinnamon extracts or other spice compounds, and brands rarely disclose which. If you want the spice to taste stronger, adding a pinch of Ceylon to any of these powders works better than hunting for a label that lists more of it.
          </p>
        </div>

