        <div id="dairy-free" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is There a Dairy-Free or Lactose-Free Horchata Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Orgain Organic Protein Horchata is the only dairy-free horchata protein powder we found on Amazon. Orgain says it is made without dairy, lactose or soy ingredients, and the protein comes from organic pea, brown rice and mung bean protein with chia seed. It is also vegan, which makes it the obvious pick for a milk allergy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The whey options vary in how much lactose they carry. LGXNDS's Horchata adds instant non-fat dry milk, and non-fat dry milk is roughly half lactose, so it is the wrong pick if you chose LGXNDS for its pure-isolate flavors. Primeval Labs uses whey concentrate, non-fat dry milk and sodium caseinate. MusclePharm Combat uses whey concentrate and milk protein concentrate but adds lactase, the enzyme that breaks down lactose, which may help mild intolerance. ProDough's other whey panels include non-fat milk powder alongside the isolate and a lactase enzyme; we could not confirm the Horchata panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lactose intolerance and milk allergy are different problems. Intolerance is about the dose of milk sugar, and many people handle a scoop of isolate-based whey without trouble. An allergy is a reaction to milk proteins, including whey and casein, so every whey horchata powder is off the table. MusclePharm Combat also contains egg and soy lecithin, which matters for those allergies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Orgain's trade-offs are protein density and sodium. Two scoops give 21 g of protein, against 25 to 26 g for the whey picks, and 430 mg of sodium. Buyers often describe plant protein as thicker and slightly gritty compared with whey, so blending it or using it in oats helps. Our <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain review</a> and <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein guide</a> cover more options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Horchata Protein Powders Avoid Sucralose?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Orgain and ProDough are the two horchata picks without sucralose. Orgain is sweetened with organic stevia (Reb A), and ProDough says its whey line contains no artificial sweeteners, with stevia and Reb M on the panels it publishes for other flavors. MusclePharm and Primeval Labs use sucralose plus acesulfame potassium, and LGXNDS uses sucralose.
          </p>

          <p>
            The LGXNDS case is worth knowing. Its product page claims zero artificial sweeteners, but its published panels, Horchata included, list sucralose. As our <a href="/lgxnds-protein-powder-review/">LGXNDS review</a> notes, if avoiding sucralose is the reason you are reading labels, trust the ingredient list over the bullet points.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweetener choice changes taste as well as label preference. Buyers commonly describe sucralose blends as sweeter and more dessert-like, which suits a flavor modeled on a sugary drink. Stevia can leave a slight bitter or licorice note for some people, and that is the most common taste complaint in reviews of stevia-sweetened powders generally. The discontinued Jocko Molk Horchata used allulose, Reb M and monk fruit, which is part of why its loss disappointed buyers who avoid sucralose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sucralose, acesulfame potassium and stevia extracts are all permitted in foods in the US, and the amounts in one scoop are small. Choose by taste and by which ingredients you prefer to avoid. If you want neither, an unflavored or unsweetened base with cinnamon and a little sugar or maple is the only way to get there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recipes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Do You Make a Horchata Protein Shake?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The simplest horchata protein shake is one scoop of horchata powder shaken with 8 to 10 oz of cold milk or rice milk and poured over ice. Rice milk gets closest to the real drink, because horchata is a rice drink at heart; almond milk is a common second choice and keeps calories down.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few changes make it taste more like the drink. A pinch of Ceylon cinnamon on top adds the aroma that a powder alone often lacks. A splash of vanilla extract rounds it out. Blending with ice gives the frothy texture, and a scoop stirred into cold milk and topped with iced coffee makes a quick horchata latte. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-coffee/">protein powder for coffee guide</a> explains how to keep whey from clumping in hot drinks: mix it into cold milk first, then add the coffee.
          </p>

          <p>
            Horchata powders also work in overnight oats, protein pancakes and mug cakes. Whey-based powders behave better in baking than plant blends, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">protein powder for baking guide</a> covers what heat does to each type. The creamer-heavy Primeval Labs formula is the one most likely to taste like dessert in oats.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also make your own horchata protein. Mix a vanilla whey with about a quarter teaspoon of Ceylon cinnamon and shake it with rice milk. This costs less than any branded horchata powder if you start from an inexpensive vanilla, such as a store brand like Walmart's Equate, covered in our <a href="/equate-protein-powder/">Equate protein powder guide</a>, or one of the picks in our <a href="/best-vanilla-protein-powder/">vanilla protein powder guide</a>. For a fully homemade route, see <a href="/homemade-protein-powder/">how to make homemade protein powder</a>.
          </p>

